  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nWhat is the deadline for filing an estate tax return, and is it the same as the individual income tax deadline?\n\n### Short Answer\n\n### Accounting Treatment\nFor accounting purposes, the estate tax return must be prepared based on the fair market value of the decedent's assets at the time of death. This includes all properties, rights, and obligations of the decedent, which must be reported accurately to determine the estate tax liability.\n\n### Tax Treatment\n\n### Differences and Reconciliation\nThe primary difference between the deadlines lies in the nature of the taxes. The estate tax is a one-time tax assessed on the transfer of the decedent's estate, while the individual income tax is an annual tax on income earned during the year. The estate tax deadline is fixed at six months post-death, whereas the individual income tax deadline is based on the calendar year.\n\n### Practical Note\nIt is crucial for executors or administrators of an estate to be aware of the six-month deadline for filing the estate tax return to avoid penalties. Additionally, they should ensure that all necessary documentation and valuations are completed in a timely manner to facilitate compliance with the tax obligations.",
